  "result": "The gate returned **OK** on the first run (1 of 6 used): all 7 witnesses passed, it composes no other rule, and I declared one narrowing.\n\n**The rule** (`LVMC_11_30_090.lean`):\n- **Who it covers:** the subject must belong to the pedestrian aggregate `DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian`, written as the aggregate id, not as a list of classes.\n- **When it applies:** the subject is on a roadway that lies on a highway, and a sidewalk lies on that same highway.\n- **What it requires:** for every roadway and highway that meet that condition, the subject must be one of:\n  - at rest (`drv_motion_complete_halt`), or\n  - moving across the roadway itself, or\n  - moving across a lane it is in that lies on the same highway (`drv_motion_moving_across_carrier`).\n\n  Otherwise it is walking along the roadway, which is a violation.\n\n**One choice the node left open:** the node says the pedestrian must not move across \"a lane or roadway carrier c\", but it doesn't say whether crossing one of those carriers is enough or all of them must be crossed. I chose \"crossing any one of them counts as crossing.\" So a moving pedestrian is in violation only when it moves along every such carrier. This is recorded as a `reading` narrowing on s3 and explained in the file header.\n\n**Witnesses:** the six planned scenes, built on a highway that holds an eastbound roadway, a lane and a sidewalk. I added one more, `branch_3`: the pedestrian crosses the lane on a roadway that has no designated direction, so the lane alone has to decide the crossing. Without it, the lane checks would never change any scene's verdict.\n\n`task_result.md` is written in the working directory.",
